# Findings — DNS Incident Analysis

## Protocol Identification

- Protocol involved: UDP (User Datagram Protocol) on Port 53 — standard DNS port
- Query type: A record lookup for yummyrecipesforme.com
- Client: Internal host 192.51.100.15
- DNS Server: External server 203.0.113.2

## What the Logs Showed

### The Request
The client sent a UDP DNS query to 203.0.113.2 on port 53, requesting the IP address associated with yummyrecipesforme.com. This is the normal first step when loading any website.

### The Response — Error, Not DNS Reply
Instead of receiving a DNS response containing the website's IP address, the client received an ICMP destination unreachable error with the message udp port 53 unreachable.

This is significant: the error did not come from inside the DNS server's application layer — it came from the network stack, indicating that nothing is listening on port 53 on that server at all.

### Persistence of the Error
The same ICMP error was returned at three separate timestamps:
- 13:24 — first failure
- 13:26 — repeat failure
- 13:28 — repeat failure

This rules out transient packet loss or a brief service hiccup. The failure is persistent and systematic.

## Impact

- DNS resolution for yummyrecipesforme.com is completely broken
- No IP address can be returned to clients
- Website is unreachable for all users, not just some
- Users see destination port unreachable instead of the page

## Likely Causes (In Order of Priority)

| # | Cause | Likelihood | Why |
| 1 | DNS service (named/bind9) stopped or crashed | Most likely | ICMP error is consistent with a stopped service, not a network block |
| 2 | Firewall rule blocking UDP port 53 | Possible | Could explain port unreachable if a recent rule change occurred |
| 3 | Server fully offline (OS/hardware failure) | Less likely | Server is responding (it returned ICMP), so it is reachable |

## Key Technical Concepts Applied

- UDP — connectionless protocol used for DNS queries; fast but no built-in error recovery
- DNS — translates domain names to IP addresses; without it, no website is reachable by name
- ICMP — used by the network to report errors; `port unreachable` means nothing is listening on that port
- Port 53 — the standard well-known port for DNS; if it is closed, DNS stops working entirely
